WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.543
When Laurie got home from school, she ran straight to the mailbox.

2
00:00:06.544 --> 00:00:13.901
Grandpa had gone to the Southwest a week ago but hadn't sent her anything yet.

3
00:00:13.902 --> 00:00:23.209
Had he forgotten about her? She saw a postcard and a yellow envelope addressed to her. "Finally!" she thought.

4
00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:30.231
Hi, Laurie! How's my favorite granddaughter? I made it to New Mexico.

5
00:00:30.232 --> 00:00:42.589
I'm in Chaco Culture National Historical Park studying the ancient Pueblos—Native Americans who lived in the Southwest over 1,500 years ago. Many

6
00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:50.470
different peoples lived here, but as a group, we call them the Pueblos, after the houses they built. Their

7
00:00:50.471 --> 00:00:56.202
houses had connecting walls and rooms built on top of one another. They

8
00:00:56.203 --> 00:01:01.167
were really more like villages than individual houses. The

9
00:01:01.168 --> 00:01:06.244
biggest Pueblo settlements are found here in Chaco Canyon. We

10
00:01:06.245 --> 00:01:10.610
archaeologists call them the great houses. One

11
00:01:10.611 --> 00:01:19.768
house, Pueblo Bonito, covered about three acres, and probably housed about twelve hundred people. Pueblo

12
00:01:19.769 --> 00:01:26.769
society had no leaders, so living together took a lot of organization and patience. But

13
00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:33.255
amazingly, they were one of the most peaceful people this country has ever known. I'll

14
00:01:33.256 --> 00:01:38.230
write you a letter explaining more when I set up camp tonight. But

15
00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:43.398
I had to send you a postcard of the amazing Pueblo Bonito! Laurie

16
00:01:43.399 --> 00:01:46.828
examined the image on the postcard. All

17
00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:55.120
the walls really were connected! She tried to imagine living in a house that was connected to everybody else's. It

18
00:01:55.121 --> 00:02:00.238
would be strange to live so close to her classmates and teacher! She

19
00:02:00.239 --> 00:02:04.645
wished she could have gone with Grandpa to see the ancient sites. There

20
00:02:04.646 --> 00:02:08.120
were so many things she wanted to know. If

21
00:02:08.121 --> 00:02:14.620
the Pueblos moved into the Southwest 1,500 years ago, where were they before that? Where

22
00:02:14.621 --> 00:02:21.561
were they now? She opened the envelope and started reading Grandpa's letter. Dear

23
00:02:21.562 --> 00:02:22.779
Laurie, I

24
00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.672
know you wish you were here and I do too. I

25
00:02:26.673 --> 00:02:34.514
bet you also have lots of questions about the Pueblos. So let's start at the beginning. Native

26
00:02:34.515 --> 00:02:43.478
Americans had been living in the United States for thousands of years before the Europeans arrived and colonized the land. Many

27
00:02:43.479 --> 00:02:51.751
historians think the natives originally came from Asia and arrived in North America during the Ice Age. Back

28
00:02:51.752 --> 00:02:56.694
then a land bridge connected Asia to Alaska. Native

29
00:02:56.695 --> 00:03:05.165
Americans might have crossed this land bridge while following bison and other large mammals they hunted. But

30
00:03:05.166 --> 00:03:09.487
Pueblos have their own version of how they got here. They

31
00:03:09.488 --> 00:03:17.912
believe they came from the earth, in a holy place underground, and were then led to the surface by a god. Origins

32
00:03:17.913 --> 00:03:25.463
aside, we know for sure that the first Pueblos were nomadic hunters and gatherers. Eventually

33
00:03:25.464 --> 00:03:37.297
they began building pit houses. These were circular underground houses, dug five or six feet into the ground. Around

34
00:03:37.298 --> 00:03:46.538
the year 700, they began building aboveground, but kept underground rooms for special religious ceremonies. Now

35
00:03:46.539 --> 00:03:53.662
their houses were made of stone and red-clay bricks, but still had no doors or windows. So

36
00:03:53.663 --> 00:04:01.503
how did the Pueblos get in and out of their houses? Through the roof!

37
00:04:01.504 --> 00:04:08.498
They climbed up a wooden ladder to the top of the wall, and then down another ladder to enter their homes.

38
00:04:08.499 --> 00:04:14.945
Imagine you're returning home after a hunt, carrying a large deer over your back.

39
00:04:14.946 --> 00:04:20.413
Or you're coming back from the spring with two large jugs full of water!

40
00:04:20.414 --> 00:04:25.137
You would become a very skilled ladder climber!

41
00:04:25.138 --> 00:04:30.549
Maybe you'd like to know a little more about the Pueblos' daily life.

42
00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:38.531
They worked very hard. Women ground corn and gathered seeds, berries, and nuts for the family meals.

43
00:04:38.532 --> 00:04:46.301
They also made pottery. Men took care of the crops, went on hunts, and wove baskets and blankets.

44
00:04:46.302 --> 00:04:50.151
Young children and girls stayed home to help their mothers.

45
00:04:50.152 --> 00:04:57.104
But older boys followed the men, learning everything they would need to know to become adults in the village.

46
00:04:57.105 --> 00:05:03.804
It was hot during the daytime, when the plateaus were lit by the sun, but nights were cold.

47
00:05:03.805 --> 00:05:10.638
The Pueblos ate warm cornbread, beans, and squash, and roasted meat over fires.

48
00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:19.903
Then they curled up in rabbit-skin robes and turkey-feather blankets and went to sleep because at sunrise they would start another long day.

49
00:05:19.904 --> 00:05:26.249
But guess what these hardworking people did in their free time, Laurie?

50
00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:34.416
They played games! Running races, dart-throwing contests, and ball games were all popular.

51
00:05:34.417 --> 00:05:42.116
Of course the games were fun, but Pueblos also believed that these games were invented by the gods.

52
00:05:42.117 --> 00:05:50.423
So playing them pleased the gods and helped keep rivers flowing, rain falling, and the sun shining in the sky.

53
00:05:50.424 --> 00:05:59.588
When I get back, let's see if playing kickball every day will keep the gods happy and bring us lots of good sledding snow this winter!

54
00:05:59.589 --> 00:06:01.753
Missing you, Grandpa

55
00:06:01.754 --> 00:06:12.247
P.S. I'll send you an email from the next site.

56
00:06:12.249 --> 00:06:21.438
Laurie ran upstairs to the computer in the study.

57
00:06:21.439 --> 00:06:26.308
In her inbox was an e-card from Grandpa.

58
00:06:26.309 --> 00:06:31.071
Laurie—I'm in Mesa Verde National Park in Colorado.

59
00:06:31.072 --> 00:06:38.073
Here Pueblos farmed, built houses, wove baskets, and performed rain dances.

60
00:06:38.074 --> 00:06:47.621
But in the 1200s, they moved down into the cliffs and built their homes into the steep walls of canyons, perhaps for protection.

61
00:06:47.622 --> 00:06:54.189
These peaceful people spent their time making pottery and offering respect to the gods.

62
00:06:54.190 --> 00:07:00.865
Then, about a hundred years later, they mysteriously left Mesa Verde forever.

63
00:07:00.866 --> 00:07:07.515
Were they escaping something or had they simply outgrown their homes? No one knows.

64
00:07:07.516 --> 00:07:17.012
They continued to live nearby until the Spanish arrived in the 1500s and forced the Pueblos to leave their homes yet again.

65
00:07:17.013 --> 00:07:21.426
But Pueblo ancestors still live in the Southwest.

66
00:07:21.427 --> 00:07:30.035
In fact, I'll meet some modern Pueblos tomorrow at a ceremony! See you soon! –Grandpa

67
00:07:30.036 --> 00:07:33.505
Laurie looked at the photograph on the e-card.

68
00:07:33.506 --> 00:07:40.338
She couldn't imagine climbing in and out of that big canyon every day just to get home.

69
00:07:40.339 --> 00:07:44.327
The Pueblos must have been so strong!

70
00:07:44.328 --> 00:07:52.507
Laurie heard the front door open. "Laurie! Are you home? Sorry I'm late!" Mom called.

71
00:07:52.508 --> 00:08:02.451
"I'm up here, Mom! I got mail from Grandpa in New Mexico!"

